---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

const title = "After marriage — lawful asset-protection tools before divorce";
const description =
  "Jurisdiction-specific educational guidance on postnuptial agreements, marital-property partitions, transmutations, separate-property records, trusts, and other lawful post-marriage planning before divorce.";
---

<SiteLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>After marriage: planning does not stop at the wedding</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A premarital agreement is not the only lawful time to classify, document, or restructure property.
    Some jurisdictions expressly let married spouses change property character, divide present or future
    community property, preserve separate-property status inside a trust, or sign enforceable agreements
    during the marriage. The correct rule is not “too late after the wedding.” It is “use the mechanism
    the governing jurisdiction actually recognizes.”
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>How this site treats jurisdiction-specific strategies</h2>
    <p>
      A strategy can appear here when there is a real jurisdiction in which the mechanism is lawful and
      supported by primary authority. That does <strong>not</strong> make it portable. Every strategy below is
      tagged to the jurisdiction that recognizes it, and the formalities, disclosure duties, fraudulent-transfer
      rules, creditor rules, tax rules, and divorce restraints of the relevant forum still control.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site will explain lawful classification, documentation, agreements, trusts, gifts, and ownership
      planning. It will not advise someone to conceal an asset, use a nominee, create a secret return agreement,
      backdate a document, misstate a financial disclosure, defeat an existing creditor, or violate a court order.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>High-value post-marriage tools</h2>

    <h3>Texas: partition or exchange community property into separate property</h3>
    <p>
      Texas Family Code § 4.102 expressly allows spouses, at any time, to partition or exchange all or part of
      existing or future community property. Property transferred to a spouse by the agreement becomes that
      spouse's separate property. The agreement can also provide that future earnings and income from the
      transferred property will be separate property. Section 4.104 requires the agreement to be written and
      signed by both spouses; § 4.105 supplies enforceability rules.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      Primary source: <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/FA/htm/FA.4.htm" rel="noreferrer">Texas Family Code §§ 4.102–4.105</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Texas: make income from separate property remain separate</h3>
    <p>
      Texas Family Code § 4.103 lets spouses agree that income or property arising from one spouse's separate
      property—whether already owned or later acquired—will itself be that owner's separate property. This can
      matter because keeping the principal separate does not automatically answer how later income is classified.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      Primary source: <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/FA/htm/FA.4.htm" rel="noreferrer">Texas Family Code § 4.103</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>California: transmute community property and separate property</h3>
    <p>
      California Family Code §§ 850–852 permit married persons to change property character: community property
      can become one spouse's separate property, separate property can become community property, and one spouse's
      separate property can become the other's separate property. The formalities are strict: § 852 generally
      requires a written express declaration made, joined in, consented to, or accepted by the spouse whose interest
      is adversely affected. Section 851 makes transmutations subject to fraudulent-transfer law.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      Primary sources: <a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=850." rel="noreferrer">California Family Code § 850</a>,
      <a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=851." rel="noreferrer">§ 851</a>, and
      <a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=852." rel="noreferrer">§ 852</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>New York: sign an agreement during the marriage</h3>
    <p>
      New York Domestic Relations Law § 236(B)(3) recognizes an agreement made before <em>or during</em> marriage
      when it is in writing, subscribed by the parties, and acknowledged or proven with the formality required for a
      deed. The agreement may address ownership, division, or distribution of separate and marital property, among
      other permitted subjects. That makes “we are already married” a timing fact, not an automatic bar to a marital
      property agreement.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      Primary source: <a href="https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/DOM/236" rel="noreferrer">New York Domestic Relations Law § 236(B)(3)</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Nevada: use a written agreement to change the default property result</h3>
    <p>
      Nevada NRS 123.070 permits spouses to contract with each other concerning property, subject to the rules that
      govern people in a relationship of confidence and trust. NRS 123.220 says property acquired after marriage is
      community property unless, among other exceptions, the spouses have an agreement in writing. Nevada also
      recognizes a separation agreement even when divorce is pending or immediately contemplated; NRS 123.080 says
      that circumstance does not itself invalidate an agreement that removes property issues from litigation.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      Primary source: <a href="https://www.leg.state.nv.us/NRS/NRS-123.html" rel="noreferrer">Nevada Revised Statutes ch. 123, especially §§ 123.070, 123.080 and 123.220</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Nevada: record a separate-property inventory</h3>
    <p>
      Nevada NRS 123.140 permits a married person to prepare and record an inventory of separate property, and
      § 123.150 gives a properly recorded inventory notice and evidentiary effects. This is a straightforward example
      of asset protection through proof rather than transfer: preserve the evidence of what is separate before a later
      classification dispute makes tracing harder.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      Primary source: <a href="https://www.leg.state.nv.us/NRS/NRS-123.html" rel="noreferrer">Nevada Revised Statutes §§ 123.140–123.160</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Nevada: preserve marital-property character inside an irrevocable trust</h3>
    <p>
      Nevada NRS 123.125 allows a trust instrument to provide that community property or separate property
      transferred into an irrevocable trust of which both spouses are distribution beneficiaries remains community
      or separate property, as applicable, during the marriage. Distributions and withdrawals covered by the statute
      can retain that character as well. A trust therefore does not necessarily have to destroy the marital-property
      classification the spouses are trying to preserve.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      Primary source: <a href="https://www.leg.state.nv.us/NRS/NRS-123.html" rel="noreferrer">Nevada Revised Statutes § 123.125</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>South Dakota: qualified dispositions and special spousal trusts</h3>
    <p>
      South Dakota has statutory qualified-disposition trusts in chapter 55-16 and special spousal trusts in chapter
      55-17. A transfer to a special spousal trust may also qualify as a qualified disposition when chapter 55-16 is
      satisfied. This is a specialized trust-planning tool, not a shortcut around marital disclosure or an existing
      claim. Chapter 55-16 expressly regulates fraudulent-transfer challenges, and the trust must satisfy statutory
      governing-law, trustee, and administration requirements.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      Primary sources: <a href="https://sdlegislature.gov/api/Statutes/55-16.html?all=true" rel="noreferrer">South Dakota Codified Laws ch. 55-16</a> and
      <a href="https://sdlegislature.gov/api/Statutes/55-17.html?all=true" rel="noreferrer">ch. 55-17</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Lower-drama strategies are often stronger</h2>
    <ul class="points">
      <li><strong>Build the tracing file now.</strong> Preserve premarital statements, inheritance records, gift letters, deeds, capitalization tables, tax basis, and the chain of substitutions when a separate asset is sold and reinvested.</li>
      <li><strong>Separate ownership from management.</strong> An entity or trust can centralize management without automatically deciding marital classification; document both questions separately.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep agreements symmetric and disclosed.</strong> The more an agreement depends on informed consent, the more important independent advice, schedules, valuation, and clean execution become.</li>
      <li><strong>Update estate planning and beneficiary designations deliberately.</strong> Marriage can change statutory rights, beneficiary expectations, homestead rights, and fiduciary roles even when title has not changed.</li>
      <li><strong>Use the jurisdiction's evidence tools.</strong> Nevada's recorded separate-property inventory is one example. In other places the equivalent may be a marital agreement, deed, trust schedule, tracing ledger, or contemporaneous valuation.</li>
      <li><strong>Revisit the plan after major events.</strong> A sale, refinance, inheritance, business recapitalization, move to another state or country, birth of a child, or change in domicile can change which rules matter.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Timing still changes the answer</h2>
    <ol class="points">
      <li><strong>Intact marriage, both spouses cooperating:</strong> this is the cleanest post-marriage window for consensual partitions, transmutations, marital agreements, trust restructuring, and documentation.</li>
      <li><strong>Separation becoming likely:</strong> stop treating a transfer as ordinary housekeeping. Get jurisdiction-specific advice, preserve records, and expect purpose, disclosure, and fairness to receive more scrutiny.</li>
      <li><strong>A claim, creditor problem, or divorce is already pending:</strong> do not assume an otherwise valid planning tool can be used retroactively to defeat an existing right. Fraudulent-transfer statutes, fiduciary duties, dissipation rules, automatic restraints, injunctions, and disclosure orders can override the ordinary planning playbook.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the product should ask before suggesting a strategy</h2>
    <ol class="points">
      <li>Where are the spouses domiciled, and where is the asset located?</li>
      <li>What is the asset's present legal classification?</li>
      <li>Is the proposed change consensual, and does the jurisdiction require independent advice or special execution formalities?</li>
      <li>Does the strategy change classification, ownership, management, creditor exposure, tax treatment, or only evidence?</li>
      <li>Are there existing creditors, claims, support duties, court orders, or a pending family case?</li>
      <li>What must be recorded, disclosed, valued, or acknowledged for the change to be effective against the spouse or third parties?</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

